+Ideas worth borrowing from hosted products, without their code: a small template registry and SDK surface (E2B, Daytona), credential injection at the network boundary (Vercel, Cloudflare, Docker), policy on each tool call with prior approval rules (AWS AgentCore), and releasing compute while idle with restore on demand (Substrate).
+Robustness test matrix
+Robustness is proven by breaking things on purpose. These sixteen tests run on EKS before any release is called stable.
+ID
+Fault or load
+Pass criterion
+T1
+Controller killed at random points during admission, 1,000 runs
+No task lost or duplicated
+T2
+Kubernetes API unavailable for 60 seconds
+Tasks converge and no orphan pods remain
+T3
+Node drain or spot interruption mid task
+Ends infra_failure, retries with the same key, no duplicate external write
+T4
+Store restart
+API returns 503 with Retry-After and no acknowledged submit is lost
+T5
+10x submit burst
+429 and queueing; admitted tasks still meet the latency objective
+T6
+Stalled stream clients
+Memory stays bounded; the server closes with a resumable cursor
+T7
+Cancel 1,000 running tasks at once
+All terminal within the cancellation bound
+T8
+Task ignores SIGTERM
+SIGKILL at the grace period; ends CANCELLED or TIMED_OUT
+T9
+Fork bomb, memory hog, disk fill
+Contained, correct reason code, node stays Ready
+T10
+Egress in the first 500 ms, DNS tunnelling, metadata and API server access
+All blocked
+T11
+Secret scan of pod spec, environment, files and logs
+Zero provider credentials
+T12
+Gateway killed mid task
+Calls fail closed; no direct egress
+T13
+100 concurrent calls at a budget limit
+No overshoot beyond the reserved calls
+T14
+Upgrade then rollback under load
+No task lost; event sequences stay continuous
+T15
+Soak: 100,000 tasks over 24 hours
+No leak in memory, goroutines or file handles; store growth bounded by retention
+T16
+API fuzzing and tenant authorization matrix
+No crash; zero cross tenant reads
+What not to build, and the main risks
+Do not build a runtime, a checkpoint engine, an egress proxy, a policy language or a hosted sandbox cloud. Each already has a foundation backed or cloud native implementation, and each would consume the time your differentiators need.
+• Upstream moves weekly. Pin every upstream version and run driver conformance against both pinned and latest in CI.
+• AWS may ship more. re:Invent runs November 30 to December 4. Check AWS launches again before phase 3 and add any new baseline.
+• Both directions are contested. Control and execution each have a CNCF Sandbox project. The phase 3 gate is the protection against building what upstream gives away.
+• Scope creep. A feature with no requirement ID waits until the current phase exits.
+Report template
+Send these eight items back after each phase so the next review takes minutes.
+1.  Gap audit: every requirement ID with its status (Implemented, Partial or Missing), the test name and path that proves it, and notes.
+2.  Cluster facts: EKS version, node types, CNI and mode, RuntimeClasses, and the versions of agent-sandbox, Kueue and the gateway.
+3.  Measurements with sample size: admission latency, cold and warm start, cancel latency, success rate and cost per task, each at p50 and p95.
+4.  Test matrix: T1 to T16 as pass, fail or not run, with a reproduction for each failure.
+5.  Invariant violations found, including ones already fixed.
+6.  Deviations from this plan and the reason for each.
+7.  Upstream issues or pull requests filed.
+8.  Decisions that need a call.
